.\" -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-
.\" The manual of remote-sync-explorer. Its Chinese translation is man/zh_CN/man1/rse.1;
.\" change both together. `rse --man` prints the one in the interface language.
.TH RSE 1 "November 2026" "rse 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rse \- remote-sync-explorer, a dual-pane terminal file manager for the local disk,
servers and rclone remotes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rse
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR KEY = VALUE ]...\&
.RB [ \-\-rclone\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-ask\-password ]
.RB [ \-T ]
.RI [ LEFT ]
.RI [ RIGHT ]
.br
.B rse \-\-forget\-password
.IB user @ host
.br
.B rse
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-licenses " | " \-\-man " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rse
shows two directories side by side, each on the local disk, on a server or on any
.BR rclone (1)
remote, and copies, moves, deletes and renames between them.
Copies and moves go into a transfer queue that shows their progress.
Between the local disk and an sftp server that has
.BR rsync (1),
rse transfers with rsync, so a changed file sends only what changed;
everything else goes through rclone, which is built into rse.
A stopped transfer goes on from where it stopped wherever the target allows it.
.PP
Remotes are those of your
.IR rclone.conf ,
managed with
.B rclone config
as usual; a server can also be opened by its address, without a remote.
.SH GETTING STARTED
.IP 1. 4
Start
.BR rse .
The left pane shows your home directory; give one or two locations to start
elsewhere:
.B rse \(ti/work sftp://me@box/srv
opens both.
.IP 2. 4
Move with the arrow keys (or
.B j
and
.BR k ),
open a directory with Enter, go up with Backspace.
Tab switches between the panes.
.IP 3. 4
To open a server in a pane, press
.B :
and type its address, such as
.BR sftp://me@box/srv ;
to open one of your rclone remotes, press
.B F10
(or
.BR g )
and pick it from the list, which also has the local disk.
.IP 4. 4
Mark entries with Space (or click them with Ctrl held) and press
.B F5
to copy them, or
.B F6
to move them, into the directory the other pane shows.
With nothing marked, the entry under the cursor is copied.
The Transfers window shows each job; Ctrl+T enters it, to cancel or retry one.
.IP 5. 4
.B ?
shows every key on the screen;
.B q
quits.
.PP
With a Chinese or Japanese input method on, the punctuation keys still work:
.BR ？ ,
.BR ： ,
.B ／
(or
.BR 、 )
and
.B 。
work as
.BR ? ,
.BR : ,
.B /
and
.B .
would.
.SH THE SCREEN
From the top:
.TP
.B the address row
over each pane: the protocol
.RB ( sftp:// ,
.BR smb:// ,
\&..., or
.B Local
on the local disk), the address bar
.RB ( [1]
and
.BR [3] ),
and a button,
.BR ↻ ,
that reloads the pane.
Enter in the focused address bar,
.B :
in a pane, or a click on the address edits it.
.TP
.B the panes
.RB ( [2]
and
.BR [4] ,
named Local or Remote after what they show): the entries under column headers
that sort them when clicked.
The pane's last row is its own line: the filter while it is typed or kept, and the
whole name of the entry under the cursor, which the list may shorten.
Marked entries are counted in the bottom right corner
.RB ( "[2 marked]" ).
.TP
.B the terminal
under the panes, while it is open (Ctrl+O; see
.BR TERMINAL ).
.TP
.B Transfers
.RB ( [5] ):
how many jobs run and wait, then a row for each.
.TP
.B the key hints
of the most used keys; click one to run it.
At its right end, in reverse video, the engine a copy between the two panes would use:
.B rsync
or
.BR rclone .
.TP
.B the message line
at the bottom: what the last action said (\(lqnothing selected\(rq, an error).
An input method shows what it is composing there.
.PP
While overwrite mode is on, a red row across the top of the screen says so.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I LEFT
The location the left pane starts in, such as
.BR ~/work .
.TP
.I RIGHT
The location the right pane starts in, such as
.BR nas:backup .
Given alone, a remote or server address opens here and the left pane shows the
directory rse was started in; a single local path opens on the left.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
rse's config file (default:
.IR ~/.config/rse/config.toml ).
.TP
.BI \-c " KEY=VALUE\fR, " \-\-set " KEY=VALUE"
A setting for this run only, such as
.BR ui.show_hidden=true ;
may be given more than once.
See
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.TP
.BI \-\-rclone\-config " PATH"
The rclone config file.
.TP
.B \-\-ask\-password
Ask for the rclone config password before starting.
An encrypted rclone config needs it before rse starts: give this, or export
.BR RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS .
.TP
.BI \-\-forget\-password " LOGIN"
Forget the ssh password remembered for
.I LOGIN
.RI ( user @ host )
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-rcd " URL"
Use an external
.B rclone rcd
instead of the built-in rclone (only in a build with the
.B rcd
feature).
.TP
.BI \-\-rc\-user " USER\fR, " \-\-rc\-pass " PASS"
User and password for
.BR \-\-rcd .
.B RCLONE_RC_USER
and
.B RCLONE_RC_PASS
are read when these are not given, and keep the password out of
.BR ps (1).
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-terminal
No terminal can be opened, locally or on a server.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the versions of rse and of the rclone in it.
.TP
.B \-\-licenses
Print the licenses of rse and of the third-party code built into it.
.TP
.B \-\-man
Print this manual, in the interface language, as
.BR man (1)
reads it (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options.
.SH LOCATIONS AND ADDRESSES
A location is a local path
.RI ( ~/x ,
.IR /abs ,
.IR relative )
or an rclone path
.RB ( nas: ,
.BR nas:backup/2026 ).
A server opens by its address, without a remote in
.IR rclone.conf :
.TP
.BR sftp://me@box/srv ", or scp's " me@box:/srv
sftp through the system
.BR ssh (1)
(see
.BR "SFTP THROUGH OPENSSH" ).
Paths are absolute;
.BR sftp://me@box/~/docs ,
or no path at all, is in the home directory, and so is scp's
.BR me@box:docs .
.TP
.BR ftp://me@box/pub ", " ftps://...
FTP;
.B ftps
is implicit TLS on port 990.
Without a user, FTP logs in as
.BR anonymous .
.TP
.B smb://me@nas/share/dir
SMB; the first name is the share.
.TP
.BR webdav://host/path ", " webdavs://...
WebDAV over http or https
.RB ( dav:// ", " davs://
too).
.PP
A port goes after the host:
.BR sftp://me@box:2222/srv .
When a login needs a password the address does not have, rse asks for it and keeps it
in memory until it quits.
Any other rclone backend opens with a connection string, such as
.BR :s3,provider=AWS,env_auth=true:bucket/path .
.PP
A name that would not show, such as one with a control character, appears with
.B �
in its place.
An entry a server lists under a name that could lead out of the directory
.RB ( .. ,
or one with
.B /
in it) is left out, and the message line says how many were.
.SH KEYS
All keys can be changed in the config file
.RB ( [keys] ).
The F keys have letter aliases for keyboards without them.
.SS The panes
.TP
.B Tab
Switch between the panes.
.TP
.BR 1 ", " 2 ", " 3 ", " 4 ", " 5
Focus a window: the left address bar, the left pane, the right address bar, the right
pane, Transfers.
The key is shown in each window's top right corner.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down ", " k ", " j ", " PgUp ", " PgDn ", " Home ", " End
Move the cursor.
.TP
.BR Enter ", " l
Open the directory.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " h
The parent directory; the cursor lands on the directory you left.
.TP
.BR Space ", " Insert
Mark or unmark the entry, and move down.
.TP
.B a
Mark all, or unmark all.
.TP
.B Esc
Clear the marks; in the address bar, back to the entries.
.TP
.B /
Filter by name, on the pane's own line: type to narrow the entries, Enter keeps the
filter, Esc clears it.
The cursor keys, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End and the wheel still move through the entries.
.TP
.BR s ", " S
Sort by name, size or time, in turn; the other way round.
.TP
.B .
Show or hide dot files.
.TP
.B :
Go to a path, a remote or an address, such as
.B nas:backup
or
.BR ftp://me@box/pub .
.TP
.BR F10 ", " g
Choose what the pane shows from a list of the local disk and the remotes in
.IR rclone.conf :
type to narrow the list, Enter opens the chosen one at its top.
With no remotes, the list says how to add one.
.TP
.B Ctrl+U
Swap the panes.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Reload the pane.
.TP
.BR F5 ", " c
Copy the marked entries, or the one under the cursor, to the other pane's directory.
.TP
.BR F6 ", " m
Move them there.
.TP
.BR F2 ", " r
Rename the entry under the cursor.
.TP
.BR F7 ", " n
New directory.
.TP
.BR F8 ", " Delete
Delete.
.TP
.B O
Overwrite mode, on or off: copies, moves and renames replace existing files without
asking.
It is off at every start.
.TP
.B Ctrl+T
Enter or leave Transfers.
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Open the terminal, or give it the keys; in the terminal, back to the panes.
.TP
.B E
The error log (the last 100).
.TP
.B ?
Help: every key, as configured.
.TP
.B q
Quit; rse asks first when that would stop transfers.
.SS Transfers
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down ", " k ", " j
Choose a job.
.TP
.B x
Cancel it.
.TP
.B r
Retry it.
.TP
.B c
Clear the finished jobs.
.TP
.BR Esc ", " Ctrl+T
Back to the panes.
.SS Dialogs
Enter presses the highlighted button, Esc cancels, and Tab, the arrow keys or a click
choose another.
In a text field, Ctrl+U clears it, and
.B <
or
.B >
at either end says the text goes on beyond it.
.SH MOUSE
Mouse support is on by default
.RB ( ui.mouse ).
A click moves the cursor there, clears the marks and focuses that pane;
a click with Ctrl held, or a right click, marks or unmarks, keeping the other marks;
a double click opens a directory, or goes up on
.BR .. ;
the wheel scrolls the pane under the pointer;
a click on a column header sorts by it, and again the other way round;
a click on blank space in a pane clears the marks;
a click on the address edits it, on
.B ↻
reloads the pane;
a click on a queue row selects the job, on its
.B ✕
cancels it;
a click on a key hint runs it.
.PP
Dragging copies or moves: press on an entry and move; marked entries move together.
Drop on a directory to put them inside it, anywhere else in a pane to put them in that
pane's directory.
A menu then offers
.IR "Copy here" ,
.I "Move here"
and
.IR Cancel ;
.B drop_action = """copy"""
copies without it.
.PP
While mouse support is on, hold Shift to select text in most terminals.
In
.BR tmux (1),
turn the mouse on with
.BR "set \-g mouse on" .
.SH TERMINAL
Ctrl+O opens a terminal across the bottom of the panes, in the directory the pane
shows: your login shell on the local disk, or on a server reached through the system
ssh, through the connection rse already holds.
Other remotes have no terminal.
While it has the keys (its border is green), every key goes to the shell, Ctrl+C and
Esc included; Ctrl+O gives them back to the panes.
When the pane goes to another directory on the same disk or server, rse types a
.B cd
into the shell, once no command runs there.
.B exit
or Ctrl+D closes it.
.SH CONFLICTS AND OVERWRITING
When the target already has an entry of the same name, rse asks: overwrite, skip,
overwrite all, skip all or cancel; skip is the default.
Names that differ only in case count as the same.
Moves and deletes are always confirmed.
Each job checks its target again as it starts, and two jobs never write one file at
once.
Overwriting goes on where it can: identical files are skipped, a half-done file is
resumed, different ones are replaced.
A stopped transfer keeps what it has done as
.I <name>.rse\-partial
(rclone) or in a
.I .rse\-partial/
directory (rsync) beside the target; delete them if you give the transfer up.
A move across remotes copies, then deletes the source.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Each setting is taken from the highest of these layers that has it: the defaults, the
config file, the environment
.RB ( RSE_ ...),
and
.B \-\-set
on the command line.
The file needs only the settings you change; a misspelt setting is an error that gives
its line and column.
The settings, with their defaults:
.PP
.EX
[rclone]
config = ""              # rclone.conf; empty: rclone's default
transfers = 4            # rclone \-\-transfers
checkers = 8             # rclone \-\-checkers
browse_threads = 2       # threads for listings and other short calls
system_ssh = true        # sftp through the system ssh

[ui]
left = "\(ti"              # left pane at start
right = ""               # right pane at start; empty: the first remote, or \(ti
show_hidden = false
mouse = true
multi_select_modifier = "ctrl"  # or "alt"
drop_action = "ask"      # or "copy"
language = "auto"        # "en", "zh\-CN", or the system's

[jobs]
max_concurrent = 2       # jobs running at once
poll_ms = 500            # progress refresh
engine = "auto"          # or "rclone": never rsync
checksum = false         # compare files by checksum

[keys]
copy = ["F5", "c"]       # any action, by its name below
.EE
.PP
The actions are named switch_pane, up, down, page_up, page_down, home, end, enter,
parent, mark, mark_all, filter, sort, sort_reverse, toggle_hidden, goto, remote, swap,
refresh, copy, move, rename, mkdir, delete, overwrite_mode, queue, terminal,
focus_left_address, focus_left, focus_right_address, focus_right, focus_queue,
error_log, help, quit, queue_cancel, queue_retry and queue_clear.
Keys are written like F5, c, E, Ctrl+U, Alt+x, Space, Enter, PgDn or Delete.
.SH SFTP THROUGH OPENSSH
rse reaches sftp servers through the system ssh, so
.I ~/.ssh/config
applies.
It logs in once per server and keeps that master connection; every connection rclone
makes goes through it, so the server sees one login per session.
The connection forwards no agent, X11 or ports.
A password, key passphrase or one-time code ssh asks for is asked in a dialog, which
can remember a password (see
.BR FILES ).
rclone's built-in ssh is used instead when there is no ssh,
.B rclone.system_ssh
is false, the remote needs something only it can do, or logging in through ssh fails;
it then checks host keys against ssh's
.IR known_hosts .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR RSE_ *
A setting:
.B RSE_
and its key upper-cased, dots made underscores, such as
.BR RSE_UI_MOUSE=false ;
a list is comma-separated:
.BR RSE_KEYS_COPY=F5,c .
.TP
.B RCLONE_CONFIG_PASS
The password of an encrypted rclone config.
.TP
.BR RCLONE_RC_USER ", " RCLONE_RC_PASS
The login for
.BR \-\-rcd .
.TP
.BR LANGUAGE ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
The interface language, while
.B ui.language
is
.BR auto :
Chinese for a
.B zh
locale, English otherwise.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME ", " XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the files below are.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/rse/config.toml
The config file
.RI ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rse/config.toml ).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/rse/queue.json
Unfinished jobs, which come back at the next start; readable by you alone.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/rse/rclone.log
rclone's log while the interface is open
.RI ( ~/Library/Caches/rse/
on macOS).
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/rse\-passwords
Remembered ssh passwords, one account a line, obscured as
.I rclone.conf
keeps passwords; used only while readable by you alone.
.TP
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/rse/ ", " /tmp/rse\-<uid>/
The sockets of ssh's master connections.
.TP
.IR <bin>/../share/man/man1/rse.1 ", " <bin>/../share/man/zh_CN/man1/rse.1
This manual in English and in Chinese, where
.B man rse
finds it:
.BR man (1)
looks beside each directory of
.B PATH
that holds programs, and shows the Chinese one in a
.B zh_CN
locale.
.I <bin>
is the directory rse is in, such as
.I \(ti/.cargo/bin
after
.BR "cargo install" .
To put both there:
.RS
.PP
.EX
man_dir="$(dirname "$(command \-v rse)")/../share/man"
mkdir \-p "$man_dir/man1" "$man_dir/zh_CN/man1"
rse \-c ui.language=en \-\-man > "$man_dir/man1/rse.1"
rse \-c ui.language=zh\-CN \-\-man > "$man_dir/zh_CN/man1/rse.1"
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when rse quits normally, 1 when it cannot start (such as for a config error) or
fails.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B rse
Your home directory on the left, the first remote on the right (with no remote, your
home directory again).
.TP
.B rse . nas:backup
The current directory and the remote
.BR nas .
.TP
.B rse sftp://me@box
The current directory, and your home directory on the server
.BR box .
.TP
.B rse \-c jobs.engine=rclone
Never use rsync, for this run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rclone (1),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5)
.PP
